1. Light out-of-range not marked red on overview card — frontend `outOfRange` RANGES lacks light. Add light range, but note light only checked during day 6-20. The card should probably mirror that. Simplest: add light to RANGES with day check? The backend checks light only 6-20. For frontend consistency, add light range and maybe apply same daytime rule. I'll add light: [2000, 80000] and only flag during 6-20 hours to match backend.

2. After manually resolving an alert, the same out-of-range condition re-creates an alert next cycle. Fix: when a manual resolve happens while condition still out of range, next simulate creates new alert since no active alert exists. Fix approach: in save_reading_and_alerts, when metric in problems and no active alert, check if there's a recently resolved alert whose condition still persists... Better approach: track "acknowledged" — when creating alert logic, don't create if the latest resolved alert for that metric/gh was resolved while... hmm.

Common approach: only create a new alert when the condition transitions from normal→abnormal (i.e., previous reading was in range), not every time there's no active alert. Implement: check the previous reading for that greenhouse (second latest). If previous reading also violated the metric, don't create a new alert (condition is ongoing). If previous was normal or doesn't exist, create.

But manual resolve + condition persists → previous reading was also out of range → no new alert. Good. When it recovers then goes bad again → previous normal → new alert. 

Edge: manual resolve, condition persists, then gets worse (level change) — still ongoing, no new alert; but bug 3 wants active alerts updated. For resolved ones, keep suppressed. That's reasonable behavior.

Implementation: in save_reading_and_alerts, for metric in problems and no active alert: query previous reading before current one for that greenhouse, evaluate whether that metric was out of range (using same thresholds, light daytime rule based on that reading's timestamp... simpler to use check_thresholds on previous reading dict). If previous also out → skip creating. Else create.

3. Worsening readings don't update active alert. Fix: if active alert exists and metric in problems, update the alert's value, level, message (keep created_at, maybe add updated_at). I'll update level/message/value on each cycle while active.

